<interrogationes unitas="chorda_aedificator">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I build a string piece by piece when I don't know its final length?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aedificator_creare chorda_aedificator_appendere_literis chorda_aedificator_appendere_chorda chorda_aedificator_appendere_s32 chorda_aedificator_appendere_i32 chorda_aedificator_finire">Create
      a builder with chorda_aedificator_creare(piscina, capacitas)
      (capacitas must be above zero or it returns NIHIL), append with
      the _appendere_ family (_literis for a C string, _chorda for a
      chorda, _character, _s32, _i32, _f64, _hex_i32, _repetita,
      _lineam_novam), then chorda_aedificator_finire returns the text
      as a chorda. The header example calls
      chorda_aedificator_appendere_integer, which does not exist: use
      _s32 for signed and _i32 for unsigned numbers (i32 is unsigned
      in the house types).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I pass the chorda from chorda_aedificator_finire to printf or fopen?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aedificator_finire chorda_ut_cstr chorda_aedificator_spectare">Not
      directly: like every chorda it carries a length and is NOT
      NUL-terminated. Print it with "%.*s" and its mensura, or copy it
      with chorda_ut_cstr(fructus, piscina) to get a C string.
      chorda_aedificator_spectare has the same shape and points at the
      same live buffer; the header calls it valid only until the next
      change to the builder.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does chorda_aedificator_finire do to the builder, and who owns the result?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aedificator_finire chorda_aedificator_reset chorda_aedificator_truncare chorda_aedificator_destruere">It
      only returns a chorda pointing at the builder's current buffer;
      nothing is copied and nothing is destroyed, whatever the header
      comment says. The bytes belong to the piscina and live until
      that piscina is destroyed or rolled back. Appending after finire
      is harmless to the result (new bytes land past its end, or in a
      new buffer when the builder grows), but chorda_aedificator_reset
      or _truncare followed by appending writes over the bytes your
      finished chorda still points at. chorda_aedificator_destruere
      does nothing.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I write a JSON string value with the builder?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aedificator_appendere_evasus_json chorda_aedificator_appendere_literis_evasus_json chorda_aedificator_appendere_character">Write
      the quotes yourself with chorda_aedificator_appendere_character
      and put the body through chorda_aedificator_appendere_evasus_json
      (or _literis_evasus_json for a C string). It escapes quote,
      backslash, \n, \r and \t, writes other bytes below 0x20 and DEL
      as \u00xx (lowercase hex), and copies bytes of 0x80 and above
      unchanged, so UTF-8 passes through without being checked. An
      empty input appends nothing and returns VERUM, also for an
      empty chorda whose datum is NIHIL.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I undo text I appended if I change my mind?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aedificator_longitudo chorda_aedificator_truncare">Save
      the position with chorda_aedificator_longitudo before writing,
      and if you don't want the result call
      chorda_aedificator_truncare with that saved length. truncare only
      ever shortens (a larger value is ignored) and keeps the capacity;
      it does not touch the indentation level. The stml writer
      (lib/stml.c) uses this for optimistic emission.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I indent nested output (pretty printing) with the builder?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aedificator_push_indentationem chorda_aedificator_pop_indentationem chorda_aedificator_indentatio_gradus chorda_aedificator_appendere_indentationem">The
      builder keeps an indentation level: push_indentationem raises it
      (capped at M), pop_indentationem lowers it (never below zero),
      and indentatio_gradus reads it. Nothing is indented
      automatically. At the start of each line call
      chorda_aedificator_appendere_indentationem(a,
      chorda_aedificator_indentatio_gradus(a)), which writes II spaces
      per level (CHORDA_AEDIFICATOR_INDENTATIO_SPATIA). reset clears
      the level too.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I rely on the b32 return value of the append functions to catch out-of-memory?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aedificator_appendere_chorda chorda_aedificator_appendere_f64 piscina_allocare piscina_conari_allocare">Not
      for memory. Creation and growth use piscina_allocare, which
      exits the process when no memory is left or a fixed-size
      piscina is full, so FALSUM never means out-of-memory (the
      builder does not use piscina_conari_allocare). FALSUM does mean
      bad input: a NIHIL builder or C string, _f64 with decimales
      above XXX or a value whose printed form is CXXXII bytes or
      longer, and chorda_aedificator_appendere_chorda on an empty
      chorda whose datum is NIHIL (what chorda_ex_literis("") returns),
      even though nothing needed appending.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why does a builder use several times more piscina memory than the final string?
    <responsum symbolum="chorda_aedificator_creare chorda_aedificator_appendere_evasus_json piscina_notare piscina_reficere chorda_transcribere">A
      piscina cannot free one allocation, so each growth (the capacity
      doubles until it fits) allocates a new buffer and leaves the old
      one behind until the piscina dies: all buffers together come to
      about twice the final capacity, which is itself up to twice the
      text. The JSON escapers also allocate scratch from the same
      piscina and never give it back: six bytes per input byte for
      strings longer than LXXXIII bytes, plus a copy of every
      non-empty input in the _literis_ variant. Start with a realistic
      capacity, or take a piscina_notare mark, build, copy the result
      into ANOTHER piscina (chorda_transcribere), then piscina_reficere
      back to the mark.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
